Subject: Layout hash derived without shared scratch state; the unit registry is locked and owner-only

What this does
  - js_aot_gen_layout probed the context and runtime layouts in two static all-zero structs (c0, r0), setting a bit and scanning for it. Two runtimes on two threads did this at once, so one could scan a struct the other had just cleared and derive a different hash. The probes now use per-call heap structs.
  - aot_unit_present derives the engine hash once and prints every compared field (abi and layout, unit and engine) on refusal; refusals are counted and reported at teardown with QJS_AOT_STATS.
  - The process-wide bytecode registry (rbc_tab) is guarded by a lock (add, delete and the backtrace lookup rbc_find), and only a runtime that owns the unit (or while it is unowned) registers in it.

Why we need it
  The AOT-harness test262 gate (-t 2) printed "AOT unit refused: engine layout mismatch (unit N, engine N)" with equal numbers: the compare and the print ran the racy derivation twice. With the hash stable, both threads bind, and js_aot_bind then grew and freed rbc_tab from two threads at once (ASan: heap-use-after-free in rbc_add, "corrupted size vs. prev_size"); that was hidden while a thread was refused.

Soundness
  The hash value is unchanged for a single thread. A non-owner runtime already interprets; it now also stays out of the registry its frames must not resolve into.

